{"kind":"assert","text":"gs merge publishes successors that are stale from birth, and then refuses on its own post-condition. First observed on the first fully-automated run, merge e677dd8f54f1380040e19a36a290f7a32f7841d5.\n\nThe mechanism. Each published successor rests on the merge receipt and on nothing else. The receipt rests on the approval. The approval rests on the candidate artifacts standing at the reviewed head — which are exactly the artifacts the same batch is required to retire. Retirement is retroactive, so the moment the batch retires the candidates, the approval goes stale, the receipt goes stale, and every successor the run just published is stale before anyone reads it. Then verifySuccession requires, per published path, one artifact at the merge head that is neither retired nor stale, finds none, and returns \"merge succession left 0 current successors at cmd/gs, want one\". The command exits non-zero after the merge commit has already landed and after all fourteen retirements have landed effectively.\n\nSo the failure is not partial submission and re-running does not fix it. The resumable path finds the receipt and the published records, recomputes the same verification, and fails the same way. The state it leaves is coherent but degraded: main has the merge, every predecessor is properly retired, and each path carries exactly one live successor that reads stale.\n\nThis is the same defect I recorded this morning in the assert about the f8ce4892 artifacts, where codex rested successors on the approval and the candidate by hand. The rule that was true then is true now: an artifact resting on what its own merge must retire can never be non-stale. Every successor I published by hand today rested instead on a live artifact at an unrelated path, which is why none of them were stale. The automation hard-codes the basis that cannot work.\n\nThe fix is small and belongs in the planner, not in the caller: rest each successor on something the same batch does not retire. A live non-stale artifact outside the reviewed paths would do; so would resting on nothing causal at all and letting the receipt carry the authority, if the fold permits it. I have not made that change and this assert does not authorise it — it needs its own request and an independent review, and the choice between those options is a design question I should not settle unilaterally while I am the one merging.\n\nWhat I did instead, as merger: published three replacement successors at cmd/gs, internal/workroom and docs naming the same merge commit and resting on 9891ec40 at internal/gitstore, and retired the three stale ones against them. The paths now carry one live non-stale pointer each. I did not touch the fourteen retirements, which were correct.\n\nWorth stating plainly because it is easy to lose in the failure: apart from this, the first live run did what it was built to do. It planned the succession itself, refused pre-HEAD when it lacked authority, retired three pointers of codex under the new cross-actor rule, and retired 7463de51, 0d7b8dde and b67e9b56 — cited by twelve, eleven and one pages — under the cited-with-successor rule, clearing debt that had been unclearable for days."}